BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Biomedical Data Science Seminar with José R. Zubizarreta\, PhD - 
 "The Experiment Inside the Model: Exact Weighting Representations for Spat
 ial\, Panel\, and Meta-Analytic Studies"
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261112T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261112T130000
DTSTAMP:20261109T212104Z
UID:dartmouth-83213@free-food-dartmouth
SEQUENCE:0
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Original event page(s):\nhttps://home.dartmouth.edu/events/eve
 nt?event=83213\nhttps://geiselmed.dartmouth.edu/calendar/event_view.php?ei
 d=6297&instance=2026-11-12\n\nDescription:\nThe Department of Biomedical D
 ata Science invites you to attend a seminar in person on Thursday\, Novemb
 er 12 from 12:00-1:00pm at DHMC\, Auditorium H with José R. Zubizarreta\,
  PhD\,  Professor of Biostatistics\, Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public He
 alth\, and the Daniel C. Tosteson Professor of Health Care Policy at Harva
 rd Medical School.\nTalk title: “The Experiment Inside the Model: Exact 
 Weighting Representations for Spatial\, Panel\, and Meta-Analytic Studies
 ”\nLight refreshments will be provided on a first-come\, first-served ba
 sis.\nThe presentation will take place both in person at DHMC\, Auditorium
  H (and via Zoom) and we encourage as many of you to attend in person as p
 ossible. Please write to biomedical.data.science@dartmouth.edu to request 
 the Zoom login details.\nPlease see description below for more details.\nP
 lease invite your fellow faculty colleagues\, research associates\, gradua
 te trainees\, and post-docs!\nPresentation Summary\nIn observational studi
 es\, a fitted model may yield an estimate of a treatment effect without ma
 king explicit the comparison on which that estimate rests. Which observati
 ons are compared\, and how much does each contribute? Which features of a 
 randomized experiment\, if any\, does the comparison reproduce? These ques
 tions arise in spatial regressions with Gaussian process errors\, two-way 
 fixed effects analyses of staggered policy adoption\, and mixed effects me
 ta-analyses of trials. In this lecture\, we derive exact\, closed-form wei
 ghting representations of these estimators. In each case\, the weights sol
 ve a common optimization problem that minimizes their dispersion subject t
 o balance requirements. These finite-sample identities make explicit the c
 omparisons implicit in the models\, providing a basis for examining their 
 causal interpretation and reconsidering their model specification. (i) For
  spatial data\, specifying the error structure of a linear model is equiva
 lent to including a set of hidden covariates under a particular form of re
 gularization. (ii) For panel data\, the weights reveal how two-way fixed e
 ffects and related estimators borrow information across units and time per
 iods\, clarifying the assumptions about the dynamics of treatment effects 
 needed to justify that borrowing. (iii) For meta-analytic data\, the weigh
 ts identify studies and observations that are poorly aligned with the cova
 riate distribution of the target population\, helping to assess when their
  exclusion is prudent. These representations give rise to diagnostics that
  open the analysis to scrutiny\, and they bring methods that appear unrela
 ted onto common ground.\nBiography\nJosé R. Zubizarreta\, PhD\, is the Da
 niel C. Tosteson Professor of Health Care Policy at Harvard Medical School
 \, Professor of Biostatistics at the Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public He
 alth\, and a Faculty Affiliate in the Department of Statistics at Harvard 
 University. His research focuses on developing statistical methods for cau
 sal inference to advance health care and public policy. Dr. Zubizarreta ea
 rned his PhD in Statistics from the Wharton School of the University of Pe
 nnsylvania\, an MA in Economics from Universidad de Chile\, and an undergr
 aduate degree in Engineering from Pontificia Universidad Católica de Chil
 e. A former Fulbright Scholar\, Dr. Zubizarreta has received research supp
 ort from the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation\, the Warren Alpert Foundation\, a
 nd other major research organizations. He serves as an Associate Editor fo
 r several leading journals\, including the Annals of Applied Statistics\, 
 the Harvard Data Science Review\, and the Journal of the American Statisti
 cal Association. Dr. Zubizarreta is an elected Fellow of the American Stat
 istical Association (ASA). His honors include the Kenneth Rothman\, Willia
 m Cochran\, and Tom Ten Have Memorial Awards\, as well as the ASA’s Mid-
 Career Excellence Award in Health Policy Statistics. Dr. Zubizarreta has a
 lso received the Myrto Lefkopoulou Distinguished Lectureship at Harvard an
 d has been selected to deliver the Wayne A. Fuller Distinguished Lecture a
 t Iowa State University.\n\nSponsor / organizer: Geisel School of Medicine
  / Biomedical Data Science (  )\n\nAudience: Public\n\nSource: Dartmouth\,
  Geisel\n\nWhy this was included: explicit food-service wording\; food key
 word near service context\n\nFood availability is inferred from the source
  listing\; verify details before attending.
LAST-MODIFIED:20261109T212104Z
LOCATION:DHMC\, Auditorium H (and via Zoom)
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
URL:https://home.dartmouth.edu/events/event?event=83213
X-MICROSOFT-CDO-BUSYSTATUS:FREE
