BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Psychological and Brain Sciences Colloquium
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261112T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261112T140000
DTSTAMP:20261109T212104Z
UID:dartmouth-82161@free-food-dartmouth
SEQUENCE:0
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Original event page(s):\nhttps://home.dartmouth.edu/events/eve
 nt?event=82161\nhttps://memorylab.stanford.edu/people/anthony-d-wagner-phd
 \n\nDescription:\nPlease join us for a colloquium in Moore Filene Auditori
 um on\nThursday\, November 12\, 2026\n\, beginning at\n1:10 p.m.\nThe tal
 k will be presented by\nAnthony D. Wagner\n\, Lucie Stern Professor in the
  Department of Psychology and Deputy Director of the Wu Tsai Neurosciences
  Institute at Stanford University.\nTitle:\nRemembering Events Past: Multi
 -System Interactions for Building and Retrieving Memories\nAbstract:\nEpis
 odic memory bridges the past and present\, guides perception\, thinking\, 
 and action in the moment\, and supports simulation of possible futures. Su
 ccessful building of event memories and their subsequent reactivation depe
 nd on interactions among multiple neurocognitive systems. In this talk\, I
  will discuss medial temporal lobe\, visual cortical\, and top-down modula
 tory mechanisms of memory. First\, I will set the stage by providing evide
 nce for computational differentiation within the medial temporal lobe (MTL
 )\, wherein MTL cortical and hippocampal learning mechanisms support the e
 ncoding of event features and the conjunctions of features\, respectively\
 , and thus support distinct forms of subsequent memory. I will then turn t
 o factors that influence how precisely visual cortex represents the featur
 es of events. I will draw on work in older adults\, whose feature represen
 tations are often less precise\, showing that the precision of event featu
 re representation depends on multiple mechanisms\, including top-down atte
 ntion and local processes. Finally\, I will discuss how preparatory attent
 ion modulates the representation of mnemonic goals\, and in turn impacts t
 he probability of memory retrieval. Collectively\, these findings illustra
 te the complex neurocognitive symphony that underlies learning from events
  and empowers future remembering and memory-guided behavior.\nCoffee\, tea
 \, and cider donuts will be available shortly before and after the talk.\n
 \nSponsor / organizer: Psychological and Brain Sciences Department\n\nAudi
 ence: Alumni\, Faculty\, Postdoc\, Staff\, Students-Graduate\, Students-Un
 dergraduate\n\nSource: Dartmouth\n\nWhy this was included: food keyword ne
 ar service context\n\nFood availability is inferred from the source listin
 g\; verify details before attending.
LAST-MODIFIED:20261109T212104Z
LOCATION:Filene Auditorium\, Moore Building
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
URL:https://home.dartmouth.edu/events/event?event=82161
X-MICROSOFT-CDO-BUSYSTATUS:FREE
